      "documentConcept": "Clinical rating scales and outcome measures: MDS-UPDRS",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:60f51cc904e1202b4424400703ba81c20c5427f31105e91a326b5d48153d8e1f",
      "text": "MDS-UPDRS measures non-motor and motor daily-life experiences, motor examination findings, and motor complications in Parkinson\u2019s disease. Validation supports use of its separate parts, while clinically meaningful change estimates depend on the part and direction of change. Repeated motor examinations require attention to medication and ON/OFF state. Longitudinal reliability and early-disease floor effects limit interpretation of small changes in individuals. Original UPDRS scores require calibration rather than direct substitution. Complementary instruments include the MDS Non-Motor Rating Scale and the PDQ-39 quality-of-life questionnaire.",
      "sources": [
